    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Maize multiple archesporial cells 1 (mac1) gene plays a crucial role in anther development by regulating cell proliferation and determining cell identity. Archesporial (AR) cells in the anther are hypothesized to secrete MAC1 into the apoplast during development, where it binds to leucine-rich-repeat (LRR) receptor kinases on surrounding L2-d somatic cells. To investigate the function of MAC1 in planta, the basidiomycete fungus Ustilago maydis was used to deliver MAC1 protein directly into the apoplast of anther tissues. A U. maydis construct was generated by homologous recombination to express and secrete a MAC1-mCherry-HA fusion protein into the maize anther apoplast. Confocal microscopy verified successful infections of maize by U. maydis, and the presence of mCherry signal. However, the signal is not confirmed to be MAC1-mCherry-HA fusion protein signal; Western blot data is needed to ensure that the fusion protein is not cleaved in planta by the host plant cell’s proteases.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chun, Michael B. and Walbot, Virginia and Hanawalt, Philip. (2014). Diffusion distance of MAC1 protein when secreted by Ustilago maydis in Zea mays anthers. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/vb987ry6513",
            "type": "preferred citation",
